{
  "id": "equations-and-inequalities",
  "title": "Equations and Inequalities",
  "term": 1,
  "summary": "Solve quadratic equations by factorising, completing the square and the quadratic formula; the nature of roots; quadratic inequalities; and simultaneous linear and quadratic equations.",
  "notes": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Quadratic equations"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A **quadratic equation** has the standard form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ with $a \\neq 0$. It has at most two solutions (**roots**). Always write the equation in standard form first."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Method 1: factorising"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 1",
      "problem": "Solve $2x^2 - 5x = 3$",
      "steps": [
        "Standard form: $2x^2 - 5x - 3 = 0$",
        "Factorise: $(2x + 1)(x - 3) = 0$",
        "$x = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ or $x = 3$"
      ],
      "answer": "$x = -\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x = 3$"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Method 2: completing the square"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 2",
      "problem": "Solve $x^2 + 6x - 4 = 0$ by completing the square. Leave the answer in surd form.",
      "steps": [
        "$x^2 + 6x = 4$",
        "Add $\\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2 = 9$ to both sides: $x^2 + 6x + 9 = 13$",
        "$(x + 3)^2 = 13$, so $x + 3 = \\pm\\sqrt{13}$",
        "$x = -3 \\pm \\sqrt{13}$"
      ],
      "answer": "$x = -3 \\pm \\sqrt{13}$"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Method 3: the quadratic formula"
    },
    {
      "type": "formula",
      "latex": "x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}",
      "caption": "The quadratic formula for $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 3",
      "problem": "Solve $3x^2 - 4x - 2 = 0$, correct to two decimal places.",
      "steps": [
        "$a = 3$, $b = -4$, $c = -2$",
        "$x = \\dfrac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 24}}{6} = \\dfrac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{40}}{6}$",
        "$x = 1{,}72$ or $x = -0{,}39$"
      ],
      "answer": "$x \\approx 1{,}72$ or $x \\approx -0{,}39$"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The nature of the roots"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The **discriminant** $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$ tells us what kind of roots the equation has, without solving it."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "Discriminant",
        "Nature of roots"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "$\\Delta < 0$",
          "No real roots (non-real)"
        ],
        [
          "$\\Delta = 0$",
          "Two equal, real, rational roots"
        ],
        [
          "$\\Delta > 0$ and a perfect square",
          "Two unequal, real, rational roots"
        ],
        [
          "$\\Delta > 0$ and not a perfect square",
          "Two unequal, real, irrational roots"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 4",
      "problem": "For which value(s) of $k$ does $x^2 + kx + 9 = 0$ have equal roots?",
      "steps": [
        "Equal roots: $\\Delta = 0$, so $k^2 - 4(1)(9) = 0$.",
        "$k^2 = 36$, so $k = \\pm 6$."
      ],
      "answer": "$k = 6$ or $k = -6$"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Quadratic inequalities"
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 5",
      "problem": "Solve $x^2 - x - 6 < 0$",
      "steps": [
        "Factorise: $(x - 3)(x + 2) < 0$. Critical values: $x = -2$ and $x = 3$.",
        "The parabola $y = x^2 - x - 6$ opens upwards, so it is below the $x$-axis **between** the roots.",
        "$-2 < x < 3$"
      ],
      "answer": "$-2 < x < 3$"
    },
